WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
BM25.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:07.200
A decades-old keyword scorer that still humbles expensive neural retrievers.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.133
BM25 is tf-idf grown up.

4
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:15.787
It scores a document for a query using plain word statistics.

5
00:00:15.787 --> 00:00:17.500
No training, and no GPU.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.367
It combines three ingredients.

7
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.833
Term frequency, but with saturation, tuned by the k one knob.

8
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:26.924
Inverse document frequency.

9
00:00:26.924 --> 00:00:31.367
A rare term like IMU zero four B weighs far more than the word the.

10
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:38.067
And length normalization, tuned by the b knob, so long documents don't win just by having more words.

11
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.533
Here's saturation.

12
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:43.367
The first occurrence of a word adds a lot.

13
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:44.633
The second adds less.

14
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:47.446
By the twentieth, the curve is nearly flat.

15
00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.667
Repeating a word can't game the score.

16
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:52.667
Know its strengths and weaknesses.

17
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:55.733
It wins on part numbers, error codes, and names.

18
00:00:56.033 --> 00:01:01.500
It loses when there are no shared words, like password reset against credential recovery.

19
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.033
The professional rule: if your fancy retriever can't beat BM25, ship BM25.

20
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:11.333
Here it is with rank BM25.

21
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:14.400
We tokenize the documents and build the index.

22
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.033
We score every document against the query.

23
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:21.067
And print the best match, the one containing that exact part number.

24
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.800
A real benchmark from Mission Control RAG.

25
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:32.100
On twenty test questions, BM25 scores sixty five percent hit-rate at five, and dense scores seventy two.

26
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.567
But on the six questions with error codes or unit IDs, BM25 wins six of six.

27
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:41.691
That split is why production runs hybrid.

28
00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:46.100
And did you compare against BM25 is a favorite interview filter.

29
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.230
Let's recap.

30
00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:53.500
BM25 combines saturated term frequency, rarity, and length fairness.

31
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.933
No training, no GPU, and a brutal baseline.

32
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.467
It wins on identifiers, and loses on synonyms.

33
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:02.607
If you can't beat it, ship it.

34
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:05.000
Next up: reranking with cross-encoders.
